import re

p2_prose = """<p>In 1956, George Miller proposed that immediate short-term memory capacity is constrained by "the magical number seven, plus or minus two" discrete informational slots. Miller demonstrated that this limit applies across unidimensional sensory judgments and digit span tasks, but crucially argued that the bottleneck is determined by the number of independent "chunks" rather than the raw amount of physical information (bits). Informational chunking represents an active recoding strategy whereby lower-level perceptual elements are bound into integrated, semantically meaningful higher-order configurations stored in long-term memory.</p>
<p>Subsequent cognitive research by Nelson Cowan challenged Miller's $7 \\pm 2$ estimate, arguing that classical digit span paradigms inflate apparent storage capacity through active subvocal articulatory rehearsal and automatic chronological grouping. When articulatory rehearsal, mnemonic clustering, and peripheral perceptual strategies are systematically eliminated (e.g., via rapid serial visual presentation and concurrent articulatory suppression), true "pure" working memory capacity is constrained to approximately $4 \\pm 1$ integrated units. Furthermore, Randall Engle posited that working memory capacity (WMC) reflects executive attention—the capacity to maintain task-relevant representations in an active state amidst potent distraction or interference.</p>
<p>To evaluate these theoretical models, psychologists assessed 60 healthy adults across four memory span paradigms. Participants completed: Condition 1 (standard random letter span with free rehearsal), Condition 2 (acronym-chunked letter span presenting 12 letters clustered into four well-known three-letter acronyms: e.g., FBI-CIA-IRS-EPA), Condition 3 (rapid letter span with continuous articulatory suppression to isolate core capacity), and Condition 4 (Operation Span, or O-Span, requiring participants to verify complex arithmetic equations while memorizing interspersed target letters). Performance metrics and correlation with Raven's Advanced Progressive Matrices (RAPM, assessing fluid intelligence $G_f$) are presented in Table 1.</p>"""

p_texts = re.findall(r"<p>(.*?)</p>", p2_prose, re.DOTALL)
prose = re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", " ".join(p_texts))
words = prose.split()
print("Word count:", len(words))
